A||||6||Scott_M||Alt. belt installation-Please Help||||8||
Z||000000||Scott_M||06-28-2000||06:43 PM||||I have an 87 SE 4 cyl w/AC, that I just replaced the alternator on.The problem is that I absolutly cannot get the serpitine belt back on. I need maybe another 1-2 inches to get it over the pulley.I scoured both my Haynes and Chiltons manuals for the answer but they are no help at all.I know this has been touched on a few times before but I haven't found the exact answer in previous posts.||||reg||8
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||06-28-2000||11:00 PM||||Scott, I have a 6cyl myself, but every car I've ever worked on had some way of losening at least 1 pulley to get the leaway you need. Check the AC and altinator, see if eather of them have one normal looking bolt/hole and one bolt/slot. if oyu losen up both bolts it altinator should be able to slide in. The only time I've seen on diferent then that was some screwy car someone brought in to class way back when in HS. There was like and extra 1/2 pulley that was used to adjust tension. Look arroun, there's gotta be something that'll give.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bill 88||06-29-2000||10:27 AM||||Ever time that I have had to remove the belt and change something, I have more than enough belt to get it back on.  I know that this is a dumb question, but Are you moving the idler far enough to get it on?||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||06-29-2000||02:31 PM||||The seprent can be a royal pain in the _____. get someone under the car to make sure it's really on the AC and crank pulleys.<P>also make sure you have the right belt.<P>there is a tool made for these seprent idlers that will fit in there easier and give more range of motion that a socket wrench. Get one. I am. I'm tired of having to remove the battery to get enough room for the socket wrench and pipe method.<P>was the old belt running centered on the idler? if it wasn't then the idler arm has likely gone bad. If the new belt also runs off part way off the idler then the whole idler arm needs replacement.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Scott_M||06-29-2000||07:03 PM||||What a bonehead I am. The idler arm is the round smooth pully in the middle right? If so I haven't adjusted it at all. I didn't know what it was there for. I suppose I loosen the nut in the middle to make it move? Right? Guess the heat here in S. Cali is getting to me.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bill 88||06-30-2000||10:02 AM||||I do not think that you want to loosen it.   Just put a wrench on the bolt in the middle of the pulley and move the idler to the right (rightie, tightie  -  lefty, loosie)  That should give you the adjustment that you need to get the belt on.  That is how I have done it on all my GM's.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||06-30-2000||12:46 PM||||Yes, the smooth pulley is the idler.<P><B>DO NOT remove or loosen the bolts on the arm.</B> Removal of the center bolt will likely ruin the arm. The center, spring loaded, section of the arm IS NOT servicable. Removal of the pulley bolt is ONLY required for replacement of the pulley bearing.<P>The arm is spring loaded. put a 15mm socket on the idler pulley bolt and rotate the wrench toward the battery. (The arm will move down as you do this.)||||reg||1
